// **The bedded rock of dry country.** Its four fresh tones are byte-for-byte
// `stone.ron`'s old family 2 ("warm sandstone"), promoted from a *tint on
// the one rock* to a rock of its own -- which is the whole of
// `Reports/rock-vocabulary-design-2026-08-29.md` in one file. Under the old
// scheme those colours arrived as a 2-D noise blob that cut across the
// bedding and read as camouflage; here they arrive as a bed, with a top and
// a bottom, that you can follow along a cliff.
//
// Set relative to `stone.ron`, which is the reference rock and carries the
// reasoning for every field; only the departures are argued here.
(
    name: "sandstone",
    display: "Sandstone",
    kind: Solid,
    // Porous, so lighter than stone's 2.5.
    density: 2.3,

    // **Two families of four tones**, `family * 4 + tone`, tone order
    // (mid, dark, light, darkest) as `stone.ron` numbers them.
    //
    //   0  fresh    the old family 2, unchanged. Random picks stay here.
    //   1  weathered  desert varnish: a sandstone face darkens with age as
    //      manganese and iron wash down it, so this family is *darker* than
    //      fresh -- deliberately the opposite direction to `mudstone.ron`'s
    //      weathering, so a mixed face reads as two rocks doing two
    //      different things rather than as one rock under a gradient.
    // **These numbers are derived, not eyeballed, and the derivation is the
    // only way to change them safely.** Shown the six-rock world the owner
    // said twice, unprompted, *"the color changes between layers is too much.
    // There is too much contrast. There should be more of a shared color
    // palette"*, and on the first correction *"I would drop the contrast even
    // more"*. So every colour here is the file's original pulled 86% onto one
    // shared warm-grey hue -- the mean of the six rocks' fresh families,
    // renormalised to unit brightness -- after the whole material has been
    // shifted half way toward the global mean brightness.
    //
    // **The shift is per material, never per colour.** The four tones inside
    // one rock are what make a bed legible, and `stone.ron`'s own comment
    // records that squeezing *those* made the banding vanish from the render
    // entirely. Shifting the material bodily leaves every internal spread at
    // exactly its original value and compresses only the gap between rocks,
    // which is what the complaint was about: measured, that gap went from 116
    // brightness points to 57 while no rock's own spread moved at all.
    //
    // Full account and the b50 measurements either side:
    // `Reports/worldgen-relief-2026-08-30.md` section 5.5.
    colors: [
        // 0: fresh -- stone.ron's retired family 2, entry for entry
        (150, 133, 118),
        (138, 122, 109),
        (165, 147, 130),
        (128, 113, 101),
        // 1: weathered -- varnish
        (126, 110, 97),
        (114, 100, 88),
        (143, 125, 111),
        (104, 91, 80),
        // 2: **damp** -- rock below the water table, and rock a pond or a
        // stream is standing against.
        //
        // Derived rather than eyeballed, and stated so it can be re-derived:
        // family 0 multiplied by (0.78, 0.79, 0.83). Wet rock is darker
        // because the water film kills the diffuse scatter off the grain,
        // and it is *cooler* because the loss is smallest at the blue end --
        // so a single multiplier per channel says the whole thing, and no
        // rock in the set needs its own artistic judgement about being wet.
        // It is one byte, written at genesis, exactly like the other two.
        (116, 102, 92),
        (106, 94, 84),
        (127, 113, 101),
        (99, 87, 78),
    ],
    base_colors: 4,

    // A little weaker than stone (16 / 12): sandstone makes good cliffs but
    // does not hold an overhang the way a limestone cap does.
    max_unsupported_span: 13,
    attached_span_bonus: 11,
    support_cost_below: 1,
    support_cost_beside: 1,
    support_cost_above: 3,

    blast_resistance: 0.8,

    // Blocks, not slabs and not grit: floor 2 rungs 5 is {4, 8, 16, 32, 64},
    // the same top rung as stone with the dust rung removed.
    fragment_floor: 2,
    fragment_rungs: 5,

    // A little coarser than stone's 13 -- sandstone joints stand further
    // apart than a fine-grained rock's do.
    joint_spacing: 15.0,

    breaks_into: "rubble",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,
    rock: true,
)
